\f0\fs22 \cf2 On the morning the cure came up the line, Jamison Hardy was in a meeting he was enjoying, which was rare enough that he had made a note of it in his own hand.\
The item under discussion was the protection of culturally significant landmarks, a campaign old money had been funding since before Hardy was old enough to be taken to the meetings, on the sound principle that if you call your own harbour frontage a landmark for long enough, some minister will eventually agree that the public has a duty to protect it. The landmarks in question were, without exception, houses belonging to the people around the table. The subcommittee had that morning secured a further appropriation to protect the beaches south to Coogee, on the grounds of their inestimable cultural value to a nation most of whom would never be admitted to them.\
Nothing whatsoever was being built. Nothing was being cleaned, dredged, planted or repaired. The money was going out beautifully and coming to nothing at all, and Hardy, who had a connoisseur\u8217 ?s appreciation for expenditure that left no evidence behind, was in the finest mood he had enjoyed all quarter.\
Then the presenter from the labs was shown in.\
He was a young man in a good suit that his mother had probably chosen, and he had rehearsed. He had a two-page summary, and he got through eleven words of it before the room started interrupting him with congratulations. Every disease. Not the treatment of every disease, not the management of it; the end of it. Bones that mended, organs that repaired themselves, a lifespan nobody at the labs had yet been game to put a number on. The young man said the words \u8220 ?no longer necessary\u8221 ? three times about three different industries and did not once notice himself saying them.\
The board was delighted. Two of the directors applauded. Someone said it was the finest thing the company had ever done, and meant it, and would be dead within the year of something entirely ordinary.\
Hardy went quiet.\
He was the only one in the room who owned anything. The others held positions, portfolios, options and expectations, all of which are things you hold because somebody else owns the thing behind them, and none of which had ever taught a man to do the arithmetic Hardy had been doing since he was fourteen. Hardy owned the hospitals. He owned the insurers who paid the hospitals, and the funds that owned the insurers, and a respectable slice of the pharmaceutical concerns that sold to both, and the debt of the people who could not afford any of it. He had not built that; he had inherited most of it and married a little more, and he had spent his working life keeping it in careful equilibrium, the way a man keeps a very old wall standing by never once leaning on it.\
He thanked the young man warmly, and then, because he was a courteous man and the young man had clearly worked hard, he thanked him again.\
\u8220 ?Don\u8217 ?t copy the file,\u8221 ? he said. \u8220 ?Not for the board. Not for me. Leave it where it is.\u8221 ?\
The young man said of course, sir, and glowed all the way to the lift, believing he had been trusted.\
Hardy sat through the rest of the agenda and voted correctly on all of it.}